 nonsensical to talk about knowing  your performance or improving your  performance if you don't have  measurements that you can tell whether  you've made an improvement I'm gonna  show another brief demo so all right  switch the order up on myself here a  little bit when you're talking about  measurements of performance it's really  important to understand that some  measurements are a lot more useful and  valuable than others and one of them  that can be not so helpful is an average  right this is a this is a quick graph of  two distributions of data that have the  exact same average but obviously they're  very different if you were only  measuring an average for these two  datasets they would look identical to  your reporting system you would say oh  no we have a you know average of 100  it's great but clearly the distribution  of this data is very different so what  is common in monitoring system is to  look at percentiles or quantiles where  you're essentially looking at buckets of  info  across a distribution and you're caring  about potentially what are most of our  requests doing or what is our slowest  you know 10% of all of our requests so  if you see numbers like P 99 or P 95 or  how many nines of what your latency is  at 3 9 so those kind of things these are  all dealing with quantiles  and percentiles so back to the demo  oh no my battery has died so I have a  backup so I have a device it's been  publishing except it has tuckered out on  me so the backup here is I have a have a  screenshot  I mean also see if I can't get this to  come back online while I do this but  I'll go through the screenshot first so  what we have here in the screenshot is  the measurement of the the publish  acknowledgement latency over MQTT - IOT  core and so this is a measurement that  we can't provide you on the cloud side  this can only be observed from the  device side so if a if a device is is  sending a message over mqtt we do a  couple interesting things for you on the  p